import Layout from '../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="About Me"
	description="How a mosquito bite, a broken spreadsheet, and one very annoying fly turned into Buzzyfly."
	pubDate={new Date('August 08 2021')}
	updatedDate={new Date('August 12 2026')}
	heroImage="/blog-placeholder-about.jpg"
>
	<p>
		Buzzyfly started on a July afternoon in 2021, in a sublet apartment with a window unit that
		couldn't keep up and a screen door that didn't quite close. I was three years into freelance
		design work, running everything — invoices, client onboarding, project files, the whole
		mess — out of a single Google Sheet that had grown fourteen tabs and a naming convention only
		I understood. I'd just lost a week of billing history to a broken formula, and I was rebuilding
		it by hand when a fly got in through that screen door and would not leave me alone. It landed
		on my monitor, on my coffee, on the sheet itself, mid-cell-reference, over and over, for two
		hours straight.
	</p>

	<p>
		I never caught it. But somewhere around hour two, fixing the same formula for the third time, I
		typed a note to myself at the top of a blank tab: "buzzy fly system — stop rebuilding this
		every quarter." It was meant as a joke, a little dig at both of us — the fly and the spreadsheet
		— for being equally impossible to get rid of. I didn't touch that tab again for six weeks. But
		the name stuck in a way the actual problem hadn't. Every time I opened the file, there it was,
		daring me to actually fix it.
	</p>

	<p>
		So I did — slowly, and only for myself at first. I pulled the invoicing logic out into its own
		templated system. I built a client-intake checklist that didn't depend on me remembering
		anything. I wrote down the pricing rules I'd been keeping in my head so I'd stop second-guessing
		myself on every new quote. None of it was clever. It was just the boring, repeatable scaffolding
		I'd been too busy putting out fires to build. By the end of that year my own client work took
		roughly half the time it used to, and I had stopped dreading Sundays, which is when I used to do
		the books.
	</p>

	<p>
		A few other freelancers I knew asked what had changed, and I gave two of them the templates,
		half expecting them to point out how obvious it all was. Instead one came back a month later and
		said it had saved her an entire weekend during her busiest stretch of the year. That was the
		moment this stopped being a private fix and became something worth building properly — cleaning
		up the templates, writing the parts down that had only ever lived in my head, and putting a
		name on the folder I'd already been using for a year. There was really only ever one option.
	</p>

	<p>
		Buzzyfly is still, at its core, that same annoyance turned into infrastructure: the small,
		repeatable stuff that's easy to put off and expensive to keep improvising. The <a
			href="/">Buzzyfly Digital System</a
		> on this site is the direct descendant of that first cleaned-up spreadsheet — rebuilt, rewritten,
		and tested against a lot more businesses than just mine, but built on the same idea. I still keep
		a fly emoji next to the brand name as a reminder of where it came from, and I still don't love flies.
	</p>

	<p>
		I write about what I'm building and what I'm learning in the <a href="/blog">Buzzyfly
			Dispatch</a
		> — mostly practical notes from actually running this, not theory. If you're dealing with your
		own version of that fourteen-tab spreadsheet, you're exactly who this is for.
	</p>
</Layout>
